{"name":"Film Noir","hex":"#473933","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"film noir","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A film genre characterized by low-key lighting, a bleak urban setting, and corrupt, cynical or desperate characters.","An individual film in this genre."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/film_noir"}],"entries":[{"title":"Film noir","blocks":[{"text":"A film noir is a Hollywood crime drama film that emphasizes cynical attitudes and motivations. The 1940s and 1950s are generally regarded as the \"classic period\" of American film noir. Film noir of this era is associated with a low-key, black-and-white visual style that has roots in German expressionist cinematography. Many of the prototypical stories and attitudes expressed in classic noir derive from the hardboiled school of crime fiction that emerged in the United States during the Great Depression, known as noir fiction. Film noir encompasses a range of plots, commonly featuring mysteries/crime dramas, detective fiction, and stories with themes of corruption, organized crime, redemption, cynicism, and tragedy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/BigComboTrailer.jpg/330px-BigComboTrailer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Film_noir"},{"title":"Film Noir (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Film Noir is the 17th studio album by American singer-songwriter Carly Simon, released by Arista Records, on September 16, 1997."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/ae/CS_Film_Noir.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Film_Noir_(album)"},{"title":"Film","blocks":[{"text":"A film, movie, or motion picture is a work of visual art that simulates experiences and otherwise communicates ideas, stories, perceptions, emotions, or atmosphere through the use of moving images that are generally, since the 1930s, synchronized with sound and sometimes feature other sensory stimuli."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Le_Voyage_dans_la_Lune_%281902%29.webm/330px--Le_Voyage_dans_la_Lune_%281902%29.webm.jpg","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Film"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Marlene_Dietrich_circa_1930_%28cropped%29.png/330px-Marlene_Dietrich_circa_1930_%28cropped%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Marlene_Dietrich_circa_1930_%28cropped%29.png/330px-Marlene_Dietrich_circa_1930_%28cropped%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Marlene Dietrich, an actress frequently called upon to play a femme fatale.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMarlene_Dietrich_circa_1930_(cropped).png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/3/3b/BlackMask1934Oct.jpg/250px-BlackMask1934Oct.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/3b/BlackMask1934Oct.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/3/3b/BlackMask1934Oct.jpg/250px-BlackMask1934Oct.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The October 1934 issue of Black Mask featured the first appearance of the detective character whom Raymond Chandler developed into the famous Philip Marlowe.[41]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlackMask1934Oct.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/OutOfThePastMitchumGreer.jpg/500px-OutOfThePastMitchumGreer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/OutOfThePastMitchumGreer.jpg/1280px-OutOfThePastMitchumGreer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/OutOfThePastMitchumGreer.jpg/500px-OutOfThePastMitchumGreer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Out of the Past (1947) directed by Jacques Tourneur, features many of the genre's hallmarks: a cynical private detective as the protagonist, a femme fatale, multiple flashbacks with voiceover narration, dramatically shadowed photography, and a fatalistic mood leavened with provocative banter. Pictured are noir icons Robert Mitchum and Jane Greer.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOutOfThePastMitchumGreer.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/LonelyPlaceTrailer.jpg/330px-LonelyPlaceTrailer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/LonelyPlaceTrailer.jpg/330px-LonelyPlaceTrailer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A scene from In a Lonely Place (1950), directed by Nicholas Ray and based on a novel by noir fiction writer Dorothy B. Hughes. Two of noir's defining actors, Gloria Grahame and Humphrey Bogart, portray star-crossed lovers in the film.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALonelyPlaceTrailer.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Lady_from_Shanghai_trailer_rita_hayworth6.JPG/500px-Lady_from_Shanghai_trailer_rita_hayworth6.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Lady_from_Shanghai_trailer_rita_hayworth6.JPG/1280px-Lady_from_Shanghai_trailer_rita_hayworth6.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Lady_from_Shanghai_trailer_rita_hayworth6.JPG/500px-Lady_from_Shanghai_trailer_rita_hayworth6.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rita Hayworth in the trailer for The Lady from Shanghai (1947)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALady_from_Shanghai_trailer_rita_hayworth6.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/DetourPoster1.jpg/500px-DetourPoster1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/DetourPoster1.jpg/500px-DetourPoster1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Detour (1945) cost $117,000 to make when the biggest Hollywood studios spent around $600,000 on the average feature. Produced at small PRC, however, the film was 30 percent over budget.[76]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADetourPoster1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/6/6a/StrayDogShadows.jpg/330px-StrayDogShadows.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/6/6a/StrayDogShadows.jpg/330px-StrayDogShadows.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Stray Dog (1949), directed and cowritten by Akira Kurosawa, contains many cinematographic and narrative elements associated with classic American film noir.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStrayDogShadows.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/1/1f/1BelmondoDoesBogey.jpg/330px-1BelmondoDoesBogey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/1/1f/1BelmondoDoesBogey.jpg/330px-1BelmondoDoesBogey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"As car thief Michel Poiccard, a.k.a. Laszlo Kovacs, Jean-Paul Belmondo in À bout de souffle (Breathless; 1960). Poiccard reveres and styles himself after Humphrey Bogart's screen persona. Here he imitates a characteristic Bogart gesture, one of the film's motifs.[108]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1BelmondoDoesBogey.jpg"}],"sounds":{},"pictures":[{"title":"Film noir 2 - Flickr - Joost Assink","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Film_noir_2_-_Flickr_-_Joost_Assink.jpg/500px-Film_noir_2_-_Flickr_-_Joost_Assink.jpg","ratio":1.502824858757062,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Film_noir_2_-_Flickr_-_Joost_Assink.jpg","share":0.346875},{"title":"Ardea cinerea (Grey heron) eating fish","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Ardea_cinerea_%28Grey_heron%29_eating_fish.jpg/500px-Ardea_cinerea_%28Grey_heron%29_eating_fish.jpg","ratio":1.3334522111269616,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ardea_cinerea_(Grey_heron)_eating_fish.jpg","share":0.273125},{"title":"The Indian grey mongoose (Urva edwardsii) mating 6","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/The_Indian_grey_mongoose_%28Urva_edwardsii%29_mating_6.jpg/500px-The_Indian_grey_mongoose_%28Urva_edwardsii%29_mating_6.jpg","ratio":1.5,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Indian_grey_mongoose_(Urva_edwardsii)_mating_6.jpg","share":0.221875},{"title":"Le Diamant noir - film de Alfred Machin - scénario (résumé conforme à la vue) avec photogrammes - btv1b6404972g (2 of 3)","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Le_Diamant_noir_-_film_de_Alfred_Machin_-_sc%C3%A9nario_%28r%C3%A9sum%C3%A9_conforme_%C3%A0_la_vue%29_avec_photogrammes_-_btv1b6404972g_%282_of_3%29.jpg/500px-Le_Diamant_noir_-_film_de_Alfred_Machin_-_sc%C3%A9nario_%28r%C3%A9sum%C3%A9_conforme_%C3%A0_la_vue%29_avec_photogrammes_-_btv1b6404972g_%282_of_3%29.jpg","ratio":0.7254273504273504,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_Diamant_noir_-_film_de_Alfred_Machin_-_sc%C3%A9nario_(r%C3%A9sum%C3%A